.pi/skills/qualcomm-android-flashing/SKILL.md:65:- bkerler/edl: **NOT installable via `pip install edl`** — that PyPI name is an unrelated video/monitoring tool (its setup imports `timecode`). Install from GitHub source: tarball (CN network: gh-proxy.com proxy — see china-mirror skill) -> extract -> `python3 -m venv venv-edl && venv-edl/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt` (PEP 668 hosts need the venv; pyusb imports as `usb`, verify `import usb, serial`). Commands: `edl.py -l <melf> printgpt` (real GPT incl. LUN layout), `secureboot`, `r`/`w` partitions, `rl <dir>` backup-all, `gpt <dir>` write GPT, `ws <start_sector> <file>` raw-sector write, `qfil <rawprogram> <patch> <imagedir>` QFIL-XML flash, `setactiveslot <a|b>`. qdl-rs: `qdl-rs --loader-path <melf> --storage-type ufs --backend serial --dev-path <COM> --phys-part-idx 4 write <part> <file>`.
.pi/skills/qualcomm-android-flashing/SKILL.md:72:- Lenovo `.x` files (rawprogram/patch/contents) are encrypted XML; decrypt with the XDA tool (`pip install cryptography` + decrypt_batch.bat).
.pi/skills/qualcomm-android-flashing/SKILL.md:155:Restoring super: OEM packages ship the dynamic partitions as **individual fs images** (ext4/erofs) + rawprogram XML giving LP-metadata offsets — NOT one sparse super.img. ROW zip packages encrypt those XMLs (`.x` extension); CN 7z packages historically ship plain XMLs — an old CN package's offsets can flash newer images (super content is not rollback-indexed). Alternatives to the `.x` decrypt: XDA Breed43214 tool (`pip install cryptography` + decrypt_batch.bat), or rebuild super with lpmake from the fs images (Python-lpmake recipe in `qualcomm-edl-flashing`).
.pi/skills/qualcomm-android-flashing/SKILL.md:166:- **bkerler/edl firehose sessions die after 1–2 partition writes with `USB Overflow`** ("Connection detected, quiting."). Re-trying the same command never recovers; the fix is a physical USB re-enumeration — unplug/replug the cable (device stays in EDL) — then continue the remaining writes one pair at a time. For BL-unlocked devices, prefer `fastboot flash <part>` over EDL for boot/vendor_boot/dtbo/init_boot/vbmeta writes (fastboot channel is stable where EDL firehose wedges). Note `edl w <partition> <img>` writes by GPT partition name (boot_a, not boot).
.pi/skills/qualcomm-android-flashing/SKILL.md:167:- **logfs "Start EBS" ≠ kernel handoff**: a logfs record ending at `Start EBS` with no `Load Image boot_a` line means the ABL gate rejected the boot (region lock, etc.). Capture logfs IMMEDIATELY after the first failed boot (`edl r logfs`) before retries overwrite the ring buffer; read misc (`edl r misc`) for the `ffbm-00` factory-mode marker.
.pi/skills/qualcomm-android-flashing/SKILL.md:172:- When the user names an obscure boot/tool (e.g. 'tirith' as a GRUB-like loader): verify it exists on PyPI/GitHub FIRST. `pip install tirith` pulls an unrelated remote-monitoring package; no bootloader project by that name exists — confirm before installing, then clarify with the user.
.pi/skills/qualcomm-android-flashing/SKILL.md:175:- **LTBox "Qualcomm kernel USB driver is missing" on Linux = qud-package check in kernel driver mode, NOT a real problem**: LTBox defaults `qcom_driver_mode: "kernel"` (`~/.config/ltbox/settings.json`) and warns when the `qud` package (source-compiled kernel-module .deb) is absent — even when `qcserial` is already loaded. Do NOT install qud (needs root + compile against the running kernel, risky on new HWE kernels). Fix: set `"qcom_driver_mode": "userspace"` in the settings file — userspace = plain libusb, the same transport the bkerler/edl chain uses. No root, no reboot; relaunch to verify the banner is gone. Detail: `references/community-tool-status-2026-08.md`.
